                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
          %h1#epistemic_logic Epistemic logic
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#introduction"} Introduction
              %li
                %a{:href => "#epistemic_formulae"} Epistemic formulae
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Axioms"} Axioms for epistemic logic
              %li
                %a{:href => "#models_for_epistemic_logics"} Models for epistemic logics
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#s5_modal_logic_as_epistemic_logic"} S5 modal logic as epistemic logic
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ManyPossibleWorldsReferences"} Possible-worlds vs. Many-worlds
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Epistemic logic
            is the branch of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            which is concerned with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epistemology"} epistemology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doxastic+logic"} doxastic logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
            and Artificial Intelligence.
          %p
            The key
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            for epistemic logic are forms of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity+and+possibility"} necessity and possibility
            corresponding to “It is known to be the case that” and “It may be the case that (so far as is known)”. As a form of modal logic, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            typically used is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds+semantics"} possible worlds semantics
            where the worlds correspond to maximally specific ways the world could have been. Philosophers disagree as to whether to distinguish the space of
            %em metaphysically
            possible worlds from the space of
            %em epistemically
            possible worlds (
            %a{:href => "#Kment21"}> Kment 21
            ).
          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            Epistemic modalities are captured in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epistemic+logic"} epistemic modal logic
            by interpreting “
            %em>
              necessarily
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            ” as saying
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                “It is known that proposition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                is true”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            we have “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            knows that proposition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is true”.
          %p
            In the closely related ‘provability logic’, the basic modal operator interprets as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                “it is provable that
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
          %p
            Notice that the notions of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibility"} possibility
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
            have different senses in ordinary language. For example, if we say ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is possible’, we may mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is:
            %em epistemically
            possible, not ruled out by anything I know;
            %em physically
            possible, not ruled out by the laws of physics;
            %em logically
            possible, not ruled out by the laws of logic. Some suggest that there is a further type of possibility,
            %em metaphysical
            possibility intermediate between logical and physical possibility. Metaphysical possibility would allow that different laws of physics might apply.
          %h2#epistemic_formulae Epistemic formulae
          %p
            These are variants of the formulae of the basic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal language
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_i
            since they relate to ‘knowledge’ and indicate the epistemic agent, the knower,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            operators in the standard form, and are used in preference to the dual
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lozenge
            forms since they are more immediately relevant to applications.
          %p
            More formally, we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prop
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \{1,\ldots,m\}
            = succeed "-agent" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi K
                    %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}^m_K(P)
            is given by the rules
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi i
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mi for
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi ::= p \mid \bot \mid \neg \phi \mid \phi_1 \wedge \phi_2 \mid K_i\phi for i\in A.
          %p
            We read
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi i
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_i \phi
            as ‘’agent
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            knows that
            = succeed "\u2019\u2019." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
          %p
            The converse or dual operators, denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_i
            (so that
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi i
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %mo ¬
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi i
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_i \phi = \neg K_i\neg \phi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            considers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is possible’’.
          .un_remark
            %h6#note Note
            %p The ‘agent’ terminology is extremely useful, but in pure modal logic texts is not used so much. It does provide an ‘intuition’ and an interpretation however.
          %h2#Axioms Axioms for epistemic logic
          %p
            The question then arises as to which axioms of modal logic are appropriate to the epistemic case. Answers will depend on what interpretation is given to ‘know’ in specifying how the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            behaves. E.g., there is a difference between the knowledge of a realistic, cognitively-limited agent and that of some idealized agent with boundless resources.
          %p It is generally admitted that axiom (T) should hold, here
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{T}: K \phi \to \phi,
          %p
            which states that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is known then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is true. Truth is generally taken to be a precondition of knowledge.
          %p
            A much more contentious issue in the field is whether to admit an epistemic version of axiom (4). Known as the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK+principle"} KK principle
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em KK thesis
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 4
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                  %mi K
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{4}: K \phi \to K K \phi,
          %p
            which states that when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is known to be true then it is also known that it is known. T
          %p#EvenPossible It is even possible to challenge the admission of two of the most fundamental modal axioms, axioms (N) and (K). These correspond to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi N
                  %mphantom
                    %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mphantom
                    %mo ,
                  %mtext If
                  %mphantom
                    %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mphantom
                    %mo ,
                  %mtext is a theorem, then so is
                  %mphantom
                    %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{N}
                  \\phantom{,}\colon\phantom{,}
                  \\text{If}\phantom{,}P\phantom{,}\text{is a theorem, then so is}\phantom{,}K P
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi K
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{K}: K(P \to Q) \to (K P \to K Q).
          %p
            An understanding of
            %strong N
            that it states that all theorems are known is evidently problematic. Similarly for
            = succeed "," do
              %strong K
            %em modus ponens
            to every instance of known
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to Q
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            On the other hand, we might consider epistemic logic to represent the reasoning of an ideal, logically omniscient, agent, and so admit
            %strong N
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %strong K
          %h2#models_for_epistemic_logics Models for epistemic logics
          %p
            The geometric or combinatorial semantics of epistemic models follows the same techniques of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke+frames"} Kripke frames
            as at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+models+for+modal+logics"} geometric models for modal logics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+models+for+modal+logics"} algebraic models
            are BAOs that is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+models+for+modal+logics"} Boolean algebras with operators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke+frames"} Kripke frames
            semantics is an example of
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                coalgebraic semantics
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/coalgebraic+semantics"} ?
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epistemology"} epistemology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity+and+possibility"} necessity and possibility
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epistemology+of+mathematics"} epistemology of mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+logic+K%28m%29"}> K(m)
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+logic+T%28m%29"}> T(m)
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+logic+S4%28m%29"}> S4(m)
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+logic+S5%28m%29"}> S5(m)
                \.
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p Early discussion of epistemic modal logic:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+H.+von+Wright"}> Georg H. von Wright
              , Section IV of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em An Essay in Modal Logic
          %p
            and introducing the formalization as
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K+modal+logic"} K modal logic
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K.+Jaakko+J.+Hintikka"}> K. Jaakko J. Hintikka
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Knowledge and belief: An introduction to the logic of the two notions
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/knowledgebeliefi00hint_0"} ark:/13960/t9k437s65
          %p A fairly recent book on epistemic logics and their applications (which was used for some of the material above):
          %ul
            %li
              J.- J. Ch. Meyer and W. Van der Hoek,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Epistemic logic for AI and Computer Science
          %p
            General books on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            with discussion of epistemic logic:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Patrick+Blackburn"}> Patrick Blackburn
                , M. de Rijke and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yde+Venema"} Yde Venema
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modal Logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcus+Kracht"}> Marcus Kracht
                ,
                %em Tools and Techniques in Modal Logic,
                Studies in Logic and the Foundation of Mathematics, 142, Elsevier, 1999,
            %li
              %p
                Johan van Benthem,
                %em Dynamic logic for belief revision
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.illc.uva.nl/Research/Reports/PP-2006-11.text.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Kment21
              %p
                Boris Kment,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Varieties of Modality
                %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/modality-varieties/#Dua"} SEP
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epistemic_modal_logic"} Epistemic modal logic
          %div
            %h3#s5_modal_logic_as_epistemic_logic S5 modal logic as epistemic logic
            %p
              Discussion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S5+modal+logic"} S5 modal logic
              as
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epistemic+logic"} epistemic logic
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+J.+Aumann"}> Robert J. Aumann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Interactive epistemology I: Knowledge
                %strong 28
                (1999) 263–300
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s001820050111"} doi:10.1007/s001820050111
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %blockquote
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a{:href => "#DitmarschHoekKooi08"} Ditmarsch, Hoek &amp; Kooi (2008)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                “in Aumann’s survey paper on interactive epistemology the reader will immediately recognise the system S5.”
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Y.+Halpern"}> Joseph Y. Halpern
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoram+Moses"} Yoram Moses
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em A guide to completeness and complexity for modal logics of knowledge and belief
                  %strong 54
                  3 (1992) 319-379
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0004-3702(92)90049-4"} doi:10.1016/0004-3702(92)90049-4
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
